2. Real-Life Example: Jane the Bank Employee

Imagine Jane works at a bank.

Stage 1: Jane is a Bank Teller

As a bank teller, Jane has access to basic customer information.

Her responsibilities include:

* Viewing customer account balances.
* Processing deposits.
* Processing withdrawals.

Her permissions are:

Permission	Access
View account balances	Allowed
Process deposits	Allowed
Process withdrawals	Allowed
View credit history	Not allowed
Submit loan applications	Not allowed
Manage loan applications	Not allowed

Jane receives only the permissions necessary to perform her daily tasks.

This is the Principle of Least Privilege in action.

Stage 2: Jane Gets Promoted to Loan Officer

Jane performs well and gets promoted to loan officer.

Her responsibilities change.

She now needs access to:

* Customer credit histories.
* Loan applications.
* Customer account balances.
* Loan application submission.

However, she no longer needs to process deposits or withdrawals.

Her permissions should therefore be updated.

Permission	Previous Role	New Role
View account balances	Allowed	Allowed
Process deposits	Allowed	Revoked
Process withdrawals	Allowed	Revoked
View credit history	Not allowed	Allowed
Submit loan applications	Not allowed	Allowed
Manage loan applications	Not allowed	According to role requirements

Important: When Jane changes roles, her old unnecessary permissions must be removed.

Simply adding new permissions without removing old ones creates excessive access.

This is sometimes called privilege accumulation or privilege creep.

