6. Least Privilege and Zero Trust

The Principle of Least Privilege is an important part of the Zero Trust security model.

Zero Trust follows the principle:

Never trust, always verify.

Users should not automatically receive access to every resource just because they successfully log in.

Instead, access should be based on:

* Verified identity.
* Assigned role.
* Required permissions.
* Security policies.
* Business needs.
* Context and risk.

Least privilege ensures users receive only the permissions necessary for their specific tasks.

